      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sherman, Texas, takes its name from General Sidney Sherman, a hero of the Texas Revolution.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherman%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1846 by Grayson County commissioners, Sherman became the county seat shortly after Texas joined the Union.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ci.sherman.tx.us/314/History-of-Sherman",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1848, the town was relocated three miles east to its present spot, a move that reshaped its growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherman%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mary Nash College opened in Sherman in 1877, marking the town as an early center of higher education in Texas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ci.sherman.tx.us/314/History-of-Sherman",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "General Sherman also built the Buffalo Bayou, Brazos and Colorado Railway, the first railroad in Texas and a precursor to the Southern Pacific.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ci.sherman.tx.us/314/History-of-Sherman",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Sherman boasts a long Jewish heritage, with synagogues and community centers reflecting its diverse cultural roots.",
